## Inherited Source Issues

The N20/N18/N19 prose has no function definition. The expressly permitted
read-only lookup located retained **6a N8, printed page 45**, prose indices
5,7,10. Its definition uses elements of two sets, at most one output, and
uniqueness on the actual domain; it explicitly calls a function a set mapping.
File SHA256:
`5292f911a80c2210dbac35010417e9a673fa5a7e48e0615275e988faebe73c3a`.

Therefore a point set can be the domain: P -> x(P) is a function, although it
is not injective. The numerical relation x -> y pictured by ABC is not a
function on [1,6], nor is b's number -> point correspondence: for 1<x<=3 it
has two points. This resolves the candidate's disclosed terminology question;
no math revision to 320 is needed. The original wording "a point" does not
provide a branch-selection rule; inventing one would change the relation.

N20's source justification extends its cube identity to all real pairs after
checking only a=b=0 outside a nonzero/unequal derivation. Other excluded cases
are not addressed there. This is an inherited expository gap, not a false
identity or answer failure: direct telescoping proves the identity everywhere.
It was not edited.

Definition lookup limitation: a first overly broad local prose locator emitted
